    <h2 id="chemistry-ecosystem">Aerosol Chemistry Ecosystem in Hair Products</h2>
    <p>While formulations are proprietary, the chemistry ecosystem behind cosmetic aerosol hair products is well-established and regulated. These systems involve a structured interaction between film-forming polymers, solvents, conditioning agents, pigments or absorbents, and propellants, all engineered for stability and safety.</p>
    <p>Compatibility between formulation components and aerosol hardware is critical. Instability can lead to clogging, pressure loss, inconsistent spray, or product separation over shelf life.</p>

    <table>
    <tr><th>Chemistry Domains</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Hair spray formulation chemistry</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Temporary hair colour formulation systems</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cosmetic solvent–polymer interaction science</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Surface chemistry of hair fibers</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Propellants and Spray Physics in Hair Aerosols</h3>
    <p>Propellants provide the driving force that enables atomization and delivery of hair aerosol products. Different propellant systems produce different spray characteristics, influencing droplet size, spray angle, wetness, and deposition efficiency.</p>
    <p>Spray physics directly impacts consumer perception, performance consistency, and product safety. Propellant choice is therefore a technical and regulatory decision rather than a marketing one.</p>

    <table>
    <tr><th>Propellant Influence Areas</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Spray force and atomization quality</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Drying speed and sensory feel</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Regulatory classification and transport compliance</td></tr>
    </table>

    
